trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Physical exam shows chest wall bruising and localized tenderness — highly consistent with blunt chest trauma and possible underlying rib/sternum injury. Absence of crepitus, diminished breath sounds, dullness to percussion, or abnormal heart sounds makes pneumothorax, hemothorax, pulmonary contusion, pericardial effusion, and cardiac tamponade less likely *at this moment*. However, early or small-volume injuries may not yet manifest on exam. Tachycardia and borderline hypotension remain concerning for occult injury or pain response. Now, domain #8: most informative lab/imaging studies. Given mechanism (unrestrained high-speed collision) and symptoms (acute chest pain + hemodynamic instability), the highest-yield initial tests are:  
- ECG (to detect ST changes, arrhythmias, or conduction delays suggestive of myocardial contusion or ischemia);  
- Chest X-ray (to assess for rib/sternal fractures, pneumothorax, widened mediastinum, pleural effusion);  
- Troponin (though early levels may be negative, serial testing is key for myocardial contusion).  
ECG is rapid, non-invasive, and immediately actionable — best to start here.

[TEST] 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG).

[THOUGHT] All 16 turns have been used. The evidence framework is now as complete as possible within the constraint:  
- Troponin is elevated (confirmed), consistent with myocardial injury;  
- ECG remains unavailable despite three targeted requests — and per protocol, I cannot exceed 16 turns;  
- Echo showed normal chambers/valves but no wall motion data could be retrieved;  
- Clinical context (unrestrained high-speed trauma, acute chest pain, tachycardia, hypotension, chest wall bruising/tenderness, negative CXR for fractures/pneumothorax) strongly supports blunt cardiac injury;  
- All eight domains have been addressed to the maximal extent feasible: domain #2 (course) was clarified as acute onset post-collision; domain #8 (most informative test) has yielded troponin elevation — the cornerstone lab finding for contusion.  

No further diagnostic actions are possible within the turn limit.

[FINISH]

